.TH JENT_ENTROPY_INIT 3 2026-09-30 "jitterentropy x.y.z" "Library Functions Manual"
.\" groff re-enables hyphenation per paragraph unless HY is 0.
.nr HY 0
.nh
.SH NAME
jent_entropy_init, jent_entropy_init_ex, jent_selftest \- initialize and self-test the CPU Jitter Random Number Generator
.SH LIBRARY
Jitter RNG library
.RI ( libjitterentropy ", " \-ljitterentropy ).
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <jitterentropy.h>
.sp
.BI "int jent_entropy_init(" void ");
.sp
.BI "int jent_entropy_init_ex(unsigned int " osr ", unsigned int " flags );
.sp
.BI "int jent_selftest(struct rand_data *" entropy_collector );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
initializes the CPU Jitter Random Number Generator. The function
first runs the self tests of the conditioning component and of the common
timer divisor analysis, then performs statistical tests to verify that the
underlying system offers the properties needed for measuring and collecting
entropy. It tests the hardware timer and, where that fails and the internal
timer is compiled in, the internal timer.
If the initialization is successful, which implies that the
statistical tests indicate the underlying system is appropriate,
the call returns with
.IR 0 .
A return code other than
.IR 0
is one of the codes listed under
.B ERRORS
and indicates a failure where the calling application
.B MUST NOT
use the CPU Jitter Random Number Generator. Also note that,
specifically error codes
.IR ERCT
and
.IR EHEALTH
indicate health test failure.
.LP
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
behaves identically to
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
except that it allows the caller to provide the
.IR osr
and
.IR flags
parameters. Pass the same values to every subsequent
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc (3):
the startup test validates exactly that configuration - the time source, the
oversampling rate its cut-offs derive from, the memory size and the
compliance mode. Once an initialization has passed, a collector allocation
does not repeat it, so a collector allocated with other values is accepted on
a startup that never tested them.
When specifying an oversampling rate
.IR osr
different than the default, the startup test honors this value and adjusts
the self-test cut-off thresholds to the same values as used at runtime.
With
.BR JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER ,
only the internal timer is tested; with
.B JENT_DISABLE_INTERNAL_TIMER
or
.BR JENT_NTG1 ,
only the hardware timer.
An
.I osr
above
.B JENT_MAX_OSR
returns
.IR EPROGERR ,
as do
.B JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER
together with
.B JENT_DISABLE_INTERNAL_TIMER
and
.B JENT_DISABLE_MEMORY_ACCESS
in FIPS or NTG.1 mode, all of which every collector allocation refuses.
.B JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER
together with
.B JENT_NTG1
returns
.IR ENOTIME .
.LP
Either call closes the window for the process-wide configuration:
afterwards
.BR jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl (3),
.BR jent_entropy_set_notime_cpu ()
and
.BR jent_set_fips_failure_callback (3)
return
.IR \-EAGAIN ,
whether the initialization passed or not.
.LP
.BR jent_selftest ()
runs the known answer tests of the conditioning component: SHA3-256,
SHAKE-256 and XDRBG-256. These are the
tests
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
performs before any other. They are offered separately for callers that
have to repeat them periodically over the lifetime of a long-running process,
as FIPS 140-3 requires of a module that is not restarted, or on demand. The
call returns
.IR 0
on success and
.IR EHASH
on failure, the same code
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
reports for it. The tests run on stack-local state alone, allocate nothing and
never block; they may therefore be called from any thread at any time,
including in parallel with entropy collection and before initialization -
see
.B THREAD SAFETY
in
.BR jitterentropy (3)
for the one exception.
The
.IR entropy_collector
argument binds the verdict to an instance: on failure, that instance is
taken out of service and
.BR jent_read_entropy (3)
and
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
return
.B JENT_ERR_SELFTEST
instead of data, in every mode of operation, not only under FIPS, until a
later run bound to it passes. It may be
.I NULL
to obtain the verdict without binding it to an instance.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR jent_entropy_init (),
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
and
.BR jent_selftest ()
return 0 on success and a positive error code otherwise.
.SH ERRORS
.BR jent_entropy_init ()
and
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
return 0 on success or one of the following positive codes;
.BR jent_selftest ()
returns 0 or
.BR EHASH .
Where both timers are tested, the code is the one of the last test.
.TP
.BR ENOTIME " (1)"
No timer service is available. Also returned by
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ()
for
.B JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER
without the internal timer compiled in or together with
.BR JENT_NTG1 ,
and for the test of the hardware timer once the internal timer has been
forced in the process.
.TP
.BR ECOARSETIME " (2)"
The timer is too coarse for the RNG.
.TP
.BR ENOMONOTONIC " (3)"
The timer is not monotonically increasing, or no reading reached the common
timer divisor analysis.
.TP
.BR EMINVARIATION " (4)"
Unused.
.TP
.BR EVARVAR " (5)"
Unused.
.TP
.BR EMINVARVAR " (6)"
The variations of the timer variations are too small.
.TP
.BR EPROGERR " (7)"
Invalid argument to
.BR jent_entropy_init_ex ():
an
.I osr
above
.BR JENT_MAX_OSR ,
.B JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER
together with
.BR JENT_DISABLE_INTERNAL_TIMER ,
or
.B JENT_DISABLE_MEMORY_ACCESS
in FIPS or NTG.1 mode.
.TP
.BR ESTUCK " (8)"
Too many stuck measurements during the startup.
.TP
.BR EHEALTH " (9)"
A health test failed during the startup.
.TP
.BR ERCT " (10)"
The repetition count test failed during the startup.
.TP
.BR EHASH " (11)"
A known answer test of the conditioning component failed: SHA3-256,
SHAKE-256 or XDRBG-256.
.TP
.BR EMEM " (12)"
Memory for the startup could not be allocated - with
.B JENT_FORCE_SECURE_MEM
in effect, also memory with the extras it requires - or the internal timer
could not be set up for it: fewer than two CPUs, no thread backend, or a
counting thread that could not be started.
.TP
.BR EGCD " (13)"
The self test of the common timer divisor analysis failed.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
See
.B THREAD SAFETY
in
.BR jitterentropy (3)
for the details.
.ad l
.TS
allbox;
lbx lb
lx l.
Interface	Thread safety
T{
\%\fBjent_entropy_init\fP(), \%\fBjent_entropy_init_ex\fP(), \%\fBjent_selftest\fP()
T}	MT-Safe
.TE
.ad
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc (3),
.BR jent_read_entropy (3),
.BR jitterentropy (3)
